    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Greek successor state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> founded in 1204 on the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast by the Grand Komnenoi.</li><li>Its wealth came from trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, carried by Genoese and Venetian merchants.</li><li>It survived by tribute and marriage alliances until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1461, eight years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> fell.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-megale-komnene">Theodora of Trebizond</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">John IV</a>, married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>; their daughter was the mother of Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p></aside>
