    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/david"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david.jpg" alt="David" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_David,_the_King_of_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerard van Honthorst, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> was founded by two grandsons of the Byzantine emperor Andronikos I Komnenos, Alexios and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. After Andronikos was overthrown and killed in 1185, the boys are generally believed to have been taken to safety in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, where they grew up under the protection of their kinswoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a>. In April 1204, a few days before the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, they seized Trebizond on the south-eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> with troops supplied by Georgia. Alexios proclaimed himself emperor, founding the dynasty later known as the Grand Komnenoi, while David led a rapid campaign westward along the coast through Paphlagonia as far as Heraclea Pontica. This brought the brothers into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore I Laskaris</a>, who was building the rival Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and David even sought help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperor in Constantinople. The western conquests did not last. Theodore Laskaris took back Heraclea and the Paphlagonian lands, and in 1214 the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykaus-i">Kaykaus I</a> captured Sinop, taking Alexios prisoner and releasing him only as a tributary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. What remained was a narrow Greek state along the coast between roughly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giresun">Kerasous</a> and the borders of Georgia, protected on its landward side by the Pontic Mountains.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trabzon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trabzon.jpg" alt="Trabzon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trebisonda,_ex-chiesa_della_panagia_Chrysokephalos,_oggi_moschea_fatih,_esterno_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francesco Bini, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> was a small state with a mixed population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> dominated the city and the coastal towns, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laz-people">Laz</a> communities lived in the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> and, increasingly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> pastoralists lived in the interior. The fertile coastal valleys produced grain, fruit, hazelnuts and wine, and the empire struck its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage; the aspers of Manuel I, bearing the image of the city’s patron saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenios-of-trebizond">Eugenios</a>, circulated widely around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The empire’s wealth came above all from trade. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in the second half of the thirteenth century, Trebizond became a principal Black Sea outlet for the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and other goods reached Western merchants. Genoese and Venetian traders obtained privileges and quarters in the city, the Genoese at the fortified Leontokastron, and their rivalry, sometimes violent, shaped the city’s commercial life. Travellers passed through on the way to Asia: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> returned that way in 1295, and in 1404 the Castilian envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruy-gonzalez-de-clavijo">Ruy González de Clavijo</a> landed there on his journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>’s court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumela-monastery">Sumela</a>, Vazelon and Peristereota held large estates in the mountains, and the Church of Saint Eugenios was the centre of civic religion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eugenios-of-trebizond"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eugenios-of-trebizond.jpg" alt="Eugenios of Trebizond" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eugenios-of-trebizond">Eugenios of Trebizond</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aquila,_Valerian,_Eugene_and_Candidus_of_Trebizond_(Menologion_of_Basil_II).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Authors of Menologion of Basil II (circa 985 AC, Constantinople), Byzantine manuscript illuminators[1] …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire never had a large army, and its survival depended more on geography and diplomacy than on military strength. Its core forces were the retinues of local aristocratic families, especially in the mountain districts of Matzouka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldia">Chaldia</a>, supplemented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laz-people">Laz</a> troops and foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The Pontic Mountains, crossed by a few passes, protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> from the Anatolian plateau, while the city itself stood on a high plateau between two deep ravines, with a walled citadel and lower town that resisted several attacks. Tradition records that a Seljuk siege of the city in the early 1220s failed, a deliverance later attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenios-of-trebizond">Saint Eugenios</a>. The empire suffered setbacks at Sinop in 1214 and in repeated conflicts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> chieftains who settled along its borders in the fourteenth century, sometimes raiding as far as the capital. Naval power was limited, and the Genoese were at times able to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> or attack the city during commercial disputes. In 1461 the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> brought a powerful army across the mountains while his fleet blockaded Trebizond by sea. Isolated after the surrender of Sinop and the withdrawal of its Turkmen ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>, the city surrendered after a siege of about a month.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Michael VIII Palaiologos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miniature_of_Michael_VIII.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Pachymeres, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Grand Komnenoi governed on the Byzantine model. The emperor held supreme authority, was crowned by the metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and was surrounded by officials with traditional titles such as <em>megas doux</em> and <em>protovestiarios</em>. Until the late thirteenth century the rulers of Trebizond claimed the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperator-romanorum">Emperor of the Romans</a>, which put them in competition with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> and later with the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In 1282 John II married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eudokia-angelina">Eudokia</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>, and adopted a more modest title, Emperor and Autocrat of all the East, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perateia">Perateia</a>, the last term referring to dependencies across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. Real power was shared with the great archontic families, such as the Scholarioi and the Kabasitai, whose quarrels led to a destructive civil war in the 1340s, when emperors were set up and deposed in rapid succession. Diplomacy was the core of statecraft. The emperors paid tribute in turn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Mongol Ilkhans</a> and other regional powers, and they married their daughters to neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> rulers, Georgian princes and Byzantine nobles. A chronicle by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-panaretos">Michael Panaretos</a>, an official of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond">Alexios III</a>, preserves the history of the fourteenth-century court.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="Hagia Sophia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagia_Sophia_(228968325).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adli Wahid, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> preserved a Byzantine Greek state and culture on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> for eight years after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453, longer than any other Byzantine realm. Its capital was a centre of learning and art. The church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, built under Manuel I in the mid-thirteenth century, combines Byzantine design with Georgian, Armenian and Seljuk carved decoration, and its frescoes are among the best preserved of the period. Scholars in Trebizond had contacts with the Persian world: the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-chioniades">Gregory Chioniades</a> travelled from Trebizond to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> around 1300 to study Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> and brought texts back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarion">Basilios Bessarion</a>, born in Trebizond around 1403, became a leading theologian, a supporter of the union of the churches at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Florence</a> and a cardinal of the Roman Church, whose library founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biblioteca-marciana">Biblioteca Marciana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond">Alexios III</a> endowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysiou-monastery">Dionysiou</a> monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> as its founding patron in 1374. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diplomatic-marriage">dynastic marriages</a> the Grand Komnenoi linked the Christian and Muslim worlds: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-megale-komnene">Theodora</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">John IV</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>, and her grandson was Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Trebizond lived on in Western romance as a fabled realm, and the Pontic Greek community preserved its memory until the population exchange of 1923.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond.jpg" alt="Alexios III of Trebizond" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond">Alexios III of Trebizond</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexios3_komnenos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Bender235 at German Wikipedia., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>’s decline was gradual and was shaped by changes in the surrounding world. The collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> after 1335 disrupted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> trade route on which the empire’s prosperity depended, and the civil war of the 1340s weakened the court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond">Alexios III</a> (r. 1349–1390) restored order and prosperity, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> chiefdoms pressed on the empire’s frontiers and it survived by tribute and marriage alliances. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-iii-of-trebizond">Manuel III</a> acknowledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>’s overlordship before Timur defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1402, but during the fifteenth century the rise of the Ottoman Empire left Trebizond increasingly isolated. In the 1450s the empire agreed to pay tribute to the Ottomans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">John IV</a> tried to build a coalition with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and with appeals to the West. These efforts failed. In 1461 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> marched east, receiving the surrender of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jandarids">Isfendiyarid</a> ruler of Sinop and persuading Uzun Hasan to withdraw. Trebizond, besieged by land and sea, surrendered in August 1461, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and his family were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Accused of conspiring with Uzun Hasan, David and most of his male relatives were executed in November 1463. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of the city were moved to Constantinople, and Trebizond became an Ottoman provincial capital.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, founded by Komnenian princes with Georgian help as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, preserved a Byzantine Greek state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> for over 250 years through trade, diplomacy and marriage alliances, outliving Constantinople by eight years before falling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> in 1461.</em></p>

